<!--
  ASOX Cisco IOS decoders (owner: Wazuh, W4.4). Range 1020xx.
  Copied into /var/ossec/etc/decoders/ at every manager start.

  The router (Cisco 2900) and the switch (Catalyst 2960) send syslog to the
  manager's 514/udp, decoded by Wazuh's built-in cisco-ios decoders
  (0065-cisco-ios_decoders.xml): cisco.facility, cisco.severity,
  cisco.mnemonic. For login messages (SEC_LOGIN-*) they don't extract the
  user or the source address, so brute force can't be counted per source and
  n8n gets no attacker address.

  This decoder has the same name and parent as the built-in last child,
  cisco-ios-default: decoders with the same name are siblings and all of them
  run, so it adds fields to that child's without replacing it. It matches
  only messages that carry "[user: x] [Source: a.b.c.d]".
  Samples follow Cisco's documented format; confirm with captures from the
  real devices (W4.1). Tests: tests/cases/1020-cisco.cases.
-->
<decoder name="cisco-ios-default">
  <parent>cisco-ios</parent>
  <regex>[user: (\S+)] [Source: (\d+.\d+.\d+.\d+)]</regex>
  <order>dstuser, srcip</order>
</decoder>
